= The Price Of Independence

It appears the Americans have finally decided to penalize us for our intervention in Cyprus. Citing outrage over the second Atilla Operation, the arms embargo imposed by the U.S. Congress officially came into effect on February 5, 1975.

Our military forces have been reliant on U.S. hardware for many decades. Most of our troops use U.S. weapons, our pilots fly U.S. planes, and our sailors captain ships that were once under the Stars and Stripes. However, we can no longer receive these weapons and their spare parts because Congress has decided we shouldn't.

Although the full effects won't be felt immediately, it will be a painful time for our forces. Our armed forces can still defend the republic, but all of our planes that need spare parts, our warships that need repairs, and our depleted stores of ammunition will cause us problems.

[? if cyprus_economic_sanctions_active == 1 : The Americans aren't happy with us lifting the ban on growing poppies either. This was an important source of income for Anatolian farmers, and the ban was a sign of subjugation by the previous Junta governments that we sought to change. However, it appears that lifting the poppy ban has angered the Americans even more than their half-excuse of Atilla II. The tensions with America are placing additional pressure on our finances, and now the economy is also under threat, with our foreign currency reserves experiencing further painful losses. ?]

One must wonder how an alliance can demand that we do whatever it wants. They want us to grow certain crops, buy certain weapons, and even decide whether to intervene to save our people according to their wishes. It appears the American fascination with Turkish sovereignty they so vehemently claimed to support lasts only if we abide by their orders at every turn.

[? if cyprus_economic_sanctions_active == 1 : However, we won't be able to mend our planes or fill our coffers with anger. ?]
[? if cyprus_economic_sanctions_active != 1 : However, we won't be able to mend our planes with anger. ?]
We will have to look for other sources of supply and boost our industries. But perhaps most importantly, maybe we should stop putting our defense in the hands of other countries.

We have been told for over two decades that we are safe because we are part of the Western alliance. However, we are learning that we aren't as safe as we thought.

- @post_event: Then we shall learn to stand on our own feet.
